---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Ambiguous = `I have a list of prize numbers. Let someone type a
position and tell them what is there.`;

const Questions = `Questions this brief does not answer

  - Do positions start at 1 (how people count) or 0
    (how the array is indexed)?
  - What happens at position 0?
  - What happens one past the end?
  - What if they type a word instead of a number?

MY DECISIONS
  - Positions start at 1 for the user; I subtract 1 inside.
  - Anything outside 1..length gets a message, not undefined.`;

const pool = [
  { slug: "cc-roster-print", title: "Roster Print", stars: 1, after: "1.22" },
  { slug: "cc-shopping-list", title: "Shopping List", stars: 2, after: "1.22" },
  { slug: "cc-safe-lookup", title: "Safe Lookup", stars: 2, after: "1.23" },
  { slug: "cc-grade-book", title: "Grade Book", stars: 2, after: "1.23" },
  { slug: "cc-high-score-table", title: "High Score Table", stars: 3, after: "1.24" },
  { slug: "cc-temperature-log", title: "Temperature Log", stars: 2, after: "1.24" },
  { slug: "cc-open-list", title: "Open Brief — Your Own Program", stars: 3, after: "1.24" },
];

const bonus = [
  { slug: "cc-word-frequency", title: "Most Common Value", stars: 3, after: "1.24" },
  { slug: "cc-reverse-list", title: "Reverse a List", stars: 3, after: "1.24" },
];
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge Set 4</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Complete any 5 of the 7 below.</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Set Is For</h2>

          <p>Everything here works on a <b>list</b>. One variable holding many values, walked through from one end to the other. This is the last set of the quarter and it is the one that matters most later &mdash; a list is a hard requirement of the Create Performance Task in Quarter 3.</p>

          <p>
            Same rule as always: <b>write your requirements list before you
            write any code</b>, and submit both.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="theme">
          <h2>This Set Is About Edges</h2>

          <p>A list has a first element, a last element, and a length, and almost every list bug lives at one of those three places. The briefs will not warn you.</p>

          <Code
            code={Ambiguous}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="From the Safe Lookup brief"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            That is a normal way for a person to describe what they want, and it
            is not enough to write a program from. Here is what should end up in
            your requirements list:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Questions}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Requirements — the questions half"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4"><b>That first question has no right answer and you must pick one.</b> People count from 1; JavaScript arrays count from 0; the AP exam reference sheet counts from 1. Whichever you choose, the program has to be consistent about it &mdash; and your requirements list has to say which you chose.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="challenges">
          <h2>The Challenges</h2>

          <p>Pick <b>any five</b>.</p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              pool.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-terminal text-sn-theme me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bonus">
          <h2>If Five Is Not Enough</h2>

          <p>
            These two are <b>extra credit</b>, worth 3 points each on top of the
            set. They are harder than anything in the pool and they are meant to
            be.
          </p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              bonus.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-flask text-sn-yellow me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3"><b>Most Common Value asks you to do something genuinely inefficient</b>, because you do not yet have the tool that would make it fast. Noticing that it is wasteful is worth as much as making it work.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For <b>each</b> challenge you complete, submit:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <b>Your requirements list</b> &mdash; including anything the brief
              left open and the decision you made about it.
            </li>
            <li><b>Your program file.</b></li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            This set is worth <b>10 points</b>, graded on completion. Extra credit
            is on top and does not replace any of the five.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenges
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
